# Tracer: sum/product split-last-term successor arithmetic.
# Before:
# sum(0, 0, fn(x Z) Z {x}) = sum(0, 170141183460469231731687303715884105727, fn(x Z) Z {x}) + 0
# Former behavior: the literal predecessor increment panicked with overflow checks.
# Now: that false equality fails normally; Rust tests cover both sum and product.
# Ordinary representable successors retain their existing behavior below.
# Boundary: i128::MAX must not wrap to i128::MIN in the matcher.
# Evidence: target/release/litex -lang en -strict -f examples/proof_nodes/equal/by_builtin_rule/split_last_term_checked_successor.lit
# Regression: split_successor_tests.

sum(1, 3, fn(x Z) Z {x}) = sum(1, 2, fn(x Z) Z {x}) + 3
product(1, 3, fn(x Z) Z {x}) = product(1, 2, fn(x Z) Z {x}) * 3
